tests.numbers:
Narrowbody deliveries on the brief:
- 760 in 2040 (63.3 a month);
- 860 in 2045 (71.7 a month);
- 940 in 2049 (78.3 a month).
That is +15 a month over 9 years: three steps of 5, about one every 3 years. Doctrine's fastest tempo would allow 18 steps in 9 years, so the ramp runs at a sixth of it. 2045-49 adds only 6.7 a month. Deliveries are demand-led on the board (a 2,000-aircraft market times our share). The brief does not give the 737 MAX and fps mix. None of the six KPIs is reported as a number, and none is flagged. The brief also has no in-service reliability data and reports no in-service event. On doctrine's prior of about 2.5 years from first delivery to design rate, the fps reached design rate around 2042-43 (inference). I table the KPI values and in-service reliability at the Aerospace Safety Committee; I do not invent them here.

ops.would_change_mind_if:
Four things would change my results.

(1) On the Re-engine, all of these together:
- a brief in which an A350 Re-engine is no longer plausible;
- a gain in the expected column above ε ($1B); this grid gives +0.30;
- a second widebody engine source ready by EIS;
- a date set with headroom against our +1 to +4-year prior.
That is the 2050 review, held only if the A350 has not been re-engined.

(2) A live quality, FAA, certification or supply problem in the brief, a flagged supplier-shortages KPI, or a Joint Venture engine durability or life-limited-part finding. None would change these all-hold orders. Each would go to the Aerospace Safety Committee as a live problem, move supplier funding up the ladder, and bar any new development until closed.

(3) Evidence that the Joint Venture engine cannot hold its share of fps supply. Then I would want a third source to protect the enabler, which sits at exactly two.

(4) Malave's rebuilt stress path showing net debt above $0 at any checkpoint. That would shrink the capital-return proposal, not my orders.

