frame.situation:
PUBLIC RECORD, 2045 (round 2 adjudicated)
- Airframes. NGSA (Airbus) has been in service since 2037, and fps (Boeing, 10-year ramp) since 2040. Both are on code 7, fitted CFM + P&W + RR. Both are in service, so the codes are locked.
- Rival NB engines. P&W and RR both committed to the Joint Venture in 2035, and GTF2 and UltraFan NB folded into it. The Joint Venture engine has been ready since 2036. I see no rival NB move left open that the board would price against us.
- Our programmes. The Ducted has been ready since 2036 and Embraer has counted since 2037. The GEnx upgrade has been ready since 2033 and has counted since 2042; there is no Re-engine on the record.
- Observed NB engine share (CFM / P&W / RR):
  - 76.0 / 24.0 / 0.0 to 2036;
  - 51.7 / 24.1 / 24.1 in 2037, with CFM easing to 50.5 by 2039;
  - 27.0 / 36.5 / 36.5 from 2040.
- WB: GEnx 42.0%, then 44.8% from 2042.
- Airframers on the record:
  - Boeing: debt first through its cash peak, and one step at a time with the FAA.
  - Airbus: one well-prepared programme at a time, with the A320neo and A350 first through the transition.
  - Neither talks of a Re-engine. RR says it starts no new widebody programme beside the narrowbody, and its Trent time-on-wing work is not the Trent 1000 upgrade order.

OUR POSITION (hold everything, S1, the only column)
- ΔPV -69.69 $B, yield 56.44%: engine value -63.69, R&D -4.00, strain -2.00. No new R&D is committed.
- CFM NB engines a year: 3,040 to 2036, 2,070 in 2037, then 1,079 from 2040 to the end of the window (2056). WB engines a year: 143, then 152 from 2042 (counted to 2061).
- Engine lifecycle value in 2045 is 13.9 nominal $B, against 31.3 in the status quo.
- Hold is $7.08B below last round's S1 (-62.61). The Joint Venture forming was outside last round's grid columns, and it took CFM from 31.7% to 27.0% from 2040. Our -77 booking covered it with $7.31B to spare. Planning on the low case worked [CX-0248, CX-0536].

GRID (one column, S1: rivals hold)
- Hold everything: -69.69 (56.4%). This is the best plan.
- Open Fan: -95.96 (40.0%), which is -26.27 against hold.
- Open Fan + Lobby: -91.58 (42.8%), which is -21.89 against hold.

OBJECTIVE
- NB share of at least 50% in 2040, 2045 and 2050: NOT MET. We are at 27.0% from 2040, 23 pp short, and no lever on this menu moves it.
- Open Fan launched and ready by 2050: NOT MET, and no order can meet it now. A 2045 launch is ready in 2055.

BOARD GUIDANCE (non-binding; labelled as such)
- Would approve: hold everything, with all five fields at hold. The Ducted, Embraer and GEnx can only be held.
- Would VETO:
  - `open_fan: launch` alone: -26.27, which is $18.27B beyond its $8B bill;
  - `open_fan: launch` with Lobby: -21.89, which is $10.89B beyond an $11B bill;
  - `lobby_emissions: launch`, which does nothing on its own and would be vetoed along with the Open Fan;
  - any item carrying a premium, which would be at least $21.89B against a $2.0B cap;
  - any cancel, though none is on the menu.
- Its grounds against the Open Fan:
  - the wait penalty takes our 27% NB share to zero for 2045-2056;
  - both airframes are in service long before 2055;
  - the loss is over the $15B RISE allowance;
  - with no airframer path, it is not joint practice with Safran unless we state a reason;
  - Ali's RISE gates are not closed on the record.
- What it wants to see:
  - Ali's fleet durability report, on field and turn-on, turn-off evidence, with every finding decided by a cross-functional team;
  - the joint capacity plan re-based to 1,079 NB engines a year;
  - RISE kept funded as technology;
  - a booking at or below -69.69, with the off-grid WB exposure labelled as the low case;
  - a plain objective note;
  - one Safran-gate line for a hold;
  - a public statement that replaces last round's 'last year of development' line.

