recommendations.text:
The installed fleet comes first. Watson should report to the Safety, Energy Transition & Tech Committee every quarter on the Joint Venture engine in service: NGSA since 2037, fps since 2040, and about 10,233 engines in our share by end-2044. The report should cover:
- durability in severe-environment operation;
- life-limited parts at full declared life;
- the joint support agreement;
- partner-shop and spares capacity for 1,460 engines a year.
If durability bites, retrofit the fleet first, as we did with the Trent 1000 durability package, and tell the Committee before anything is said in public. Product safety is and always must be our most important priority.

Make the partnership work, and show the board it does. Bring a Joint Venture governance note before the 2050 round. Pratt & Whitney says in public that the venture shares risk and investment, not technology, and that the gear, the core and GTF2 stay its modules, under its design authority. We said we bring a technology. The note should set out:
- design authority;
- our technology role and how it is protected;
- work share and revenue against equal narrowbody shares;
- the joint support network.
We left IAE because the partners were no longer aligned, and we will not drift there again. No Rolls-Royce money beyond our $4B share goes in without the board. Say nothing in public on the venture's terms beyond the record.

