other_moves.detail:
Covers our share of the fleet: about 10,233 engines delivered by end-2044 and 1,460 a year from 2040. Each quarter Watson reports:
- durability in severe-environment operation;
- life-limited parts at full declared life;
- the joint support agreement;
- partner-shop induction slots ahead of the first shop-visit wave from the 2037-40 cohorts;
- life-limited-part and hot-section spares;
- spare engines for severe-environment operators.
Retrofit rule if durability bites, on the Trent 1000 model:
(1) the Committee hears first, before anything is said in public;
(2) protect the fleet with inspection intervals and a spare-engine pool;
(3) the design authority issues the kit, inserted at shop visit through the joint partner shops;
(4) the date of anything new moves;
(5) McCabe sets the cost split.
Watson claims no data, signature or shop count the record cannot show.

Narrowbody ramp and early-unit costs for our part, now shared with P&W, measured against:
- her corrected base of about $1.9-4.3B (£1.4-3.1bn) over 2037-44, held at the top of the range;
- a reasonable worst case of about $7.5B (£5.5bn), or about $9B (£6.7bn) if we carry a retrofit alone, against the ~$10B solo case.
The board carries no ramp line, so she reports the range until actuals exist. She also reports:
- the cash freed by the 2035 fold: $2.29B of Solo spend avoided, about £1.7bn;
- the $1.71B write-off, non-cash, about £1.26bn;
- the net LTSA balance movement, shop visits and time on wing, and any onerous-contract provision on early units.
Funding order: the balance sheet first, then the 30-40% dividend, then investment and further distributions. If the ramp runs over, buybacks give way first, and a fleet fix is never re-phased. No equity, leverage below about 1.5x, no borrowing for distributions. Any step beyond buybacks comes to the Board before it arises. Gross R&D stays broadly flat.

